\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Hermite diagonalization}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the \vocabk{Gaussian transfer operator}{fdip-007T}, define
##{\begin{aligned}
\cosh\omega&=1+\frac{m^2}{2w},& \omega&>0,\\
q&=\ue^{-\omega},& s&=w\sinh\omega,\\
\lambda_0&=\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{w}}\ue^{-\omega /2}.
\end{aligned}}
Define the probabilists' \newvocab{Hermite polynomials}
#{\operatorname{He}_n} by the generating function
##{\ue^{uz-z^2/2}
 =\sum_{n=0}^\infty\operatorname{He}_n(u)\frac{z^n}{n!},}
and put
##{h_n^s(x)=\left(\frac{s}{\pi}\right)^{1/4}
 \frac{\operatorname{He}_n(\sqrt{2s}\,x)}{\sqrt{n!}}
 \ue^{-sx^2/2}.}
These functions form an orthonormal basis of
#{L^2(\mathbb R,\extd x;\mathbb C)}, and
##{Th_n^s=\lambda_0q^nh_n^s\qquad(n\ge0).}
Consequently #{T} is positive, injective and trace class, with
#{\|T\|=\lambda_0} and
#{\traceT=\lambda_0/(1-q)}.
The normalized operator #{\widehat T=T/\lambda_0} is an
injective positive contraction.}

\proof{
\p{First establish the basis. Integrating two generating functions
against #{\ue^{-sx^2}} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{\mathbb R}\ue^{-sx^2}
 \ue^{\sqrt{2s}\,xz-z^2/2}
 \ue^{\sqrt{2s}\,xt-t^2/2}\,\extd x\\
&\hspace{20mm}=\sqrt{\frac\pi s}\,\ue^{zt}.
\end{aligned}}
Differentiation at #{z=t=0} proves orthogonality with precisely
the displayed normalization. Every such differentiation is
justified by a polynomial times an integrable Gaussian, uniformly
for the complex parameters in a compact set.}

\p{For completeness, suppose #{f\in L^2} is orthogonal to every
#{h_n^s}. Since #{\operatorname{He}_n} has degree #{n} and leading
coefficient one, all moments of
#{g(x)=f(x)\ue^{-sx^2/2}} vanish. Cauchy–Schwarz shows that
#{g\in L^1} and that
##{F(z)=\int_{\mathbb R}g(x)\ue^{zx}\,\extd x}
is entire: on every compact set of complex #{z}, all derivative
integrands have an integrable majorant of the form
#{|f(x)|\,|x|^k \ue^{-sx^2/2+R|x|}}.
All Taylor coefficients of #{F} vanish, so #{F=0}; in particular
the Fourier transform of #{g} vanishes. To see the needed
uniqueness directly, convolve #{g} with a normalized Gaussian.
The Gaussian Fourier integral and Fubini express this convolution
as an integral of its zero Fourier transform, hence it is zero.
As the Gaussian variance tends to zero, these convolutions tend
to #{g} in #{L^1}, by continuity of translations in #{L^1} and
dominated convergence. Thus #{g=0} almost everywhere and #{f=0}.
The orthonormal family is complete.}

\p{Now diagonalize the kernel. With
#{a=w\cosh\omega /2}, the identities
#{s=w\sinh\omega} and #{a+s/2=w/(2q)} give, by completing
the square in #{y},
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{\mathbb R}K(x,y)\ue^{-sy^2/2}
 \ue^{\sqrt{2s}\,yz-z^2/2}\,\extd y\\
&\hspace{8mm}=\lambda_0\ue^{-sx^2/2}
 \ue^{\sqrt{2s}\,xqz-q^2z^2/2}.
\end{aligned}}
The constant is
#{\sqrt{\pi /(a+s/2)}=\sqrt{2\pi q/w}=\lambda_0}.
Differentiating #{n} times at zero, again under a Gaussian
majorant, gives #{Th_n^s=\lambda_0q^nh_n^s}.}

\p{If #{f=\sum_n c_nh_n^s}, boundedness and completeness give
##{Tf=\sum_{n=0}^\infty\lambda_0q^nc_nh_n^s}
in #{L^2}. Every eigenvalue is strictly positive, which proves
positivity and injectivity. Their maximum is #{\lambda_0}.
For a positive compact operator, being trace class means that
these eigenvalues have finite sum; here the sum is
#{\lambda_0/(1-q)}. This proves all operator assertions.
It also gives the Mehler kernel expansion
##{K(x,y)=\sum_{n=0}^\infty
 \lambda_0q^nh_n^s(x)h_n^s(y)}
in #{L^2(\mathbb R^2)}: the squared Hilbert–Schmidt norm of the
tail is #{\lambda_0^2\sum_{n>r}q^{2n}} after truncation at #{r}.
This convergence statement suffices for the operator identity.}
}

\p{The calculation is a direct diagonalization of
\ref{fdip-007T}. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.hermiteHilbertBasis} and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.coe_hermiteHilbertBasis}
supply the complete orthonormal Hermite basis at #{s=1}, with
exactly the probabilists' normalization used here. The unitary
dilation #{f(x)\mapsto s^{1/4}f(\sqrt{s}\,x)} gives the displayed
basis for general #{s>0}. These references support the basis;
the kernel eigenvalues and transfer properties are proved above.}
